ML_PAGE("t3-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l3\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 3 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Topic 3 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l3\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"t3-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"t3-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l3\" id=\"t3n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">63 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. Why do we need governance if we already have management?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s1\" title=\"1. Why do we need governance if we already have management?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Why governance?</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Management vs governance - one of the most important distinctions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s2\" title=\"2. Management vs governance - one of the most important distinctions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Management vs governance</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Governance and management affect each other\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s3\" title=\"3. Governance and management affect each other\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Governance and management affect each other</button></li><li data-full=\"4. The objective of corporate governance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s4\" title=\"4. The objective of corporate governance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>The objective of corporate governance</button></li><li data-full=\"5. The five elements of corporate governance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s5\" title=\"5. The five elements of corporate governance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>The five elements of corporate governance</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Accountability solves the &quot;who is responsible?&quot; problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s6\" title=\"6. Accountability solves the &quot;who is responsible?&quot; problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Accountability</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Transparency solves the information asymmetry problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s7\" title=\"7. Transparency solves the information asymmetry problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Transparency</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Regulatory framework solves the &quot;rules of the game&quot; problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s8\" title=\"8. Regulatory framework solves the &quot;rules of the game&quot; problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Regulatory framework</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Business ethics and social responsibility solve the &quot;legal but wrong&quot; problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s9\" title=\"9. Business ethics and social responsibility solve the &quot;legal but wrong&quot; problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Ethics and social responsibility</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Administrative structure solves the &quot;who reports to whom?&quot; problem\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s10\" title=\"10. Administrative structure solves the &quot;who reports to whom?&quot; problem\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Administrative structure</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Corporate governance vs risk governance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s11\" title=\"11. Corporate governance vs risk governance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Corporate governance vs risk governance</button></li><li data-full=\"12. What does &quot;embedded&quot; really mean?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s12\" title=\"12. What does &quot;embedded&quot; really mean?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>What does \"embedded\" really mean?</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Risk-management framework: the operating architecture\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s13\" title=\"13. Risk-management framework: the operating architecture\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Risk-management framework</button></li><li data-full=\"14. The seven building blocks of risk governance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s14\" title=\"14. The seven building blocks of risk governance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>The seven building blocks of risk governance</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Portfolio management is especially important\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s15\" title=\"15. Portfolio management is especially important\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Portfolio management is especially important</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Risk management and governance have a two-way relationship\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s16\" title=\"16. Risk management and governance have a two-way relationship\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Governance and RM: two-way</button></li><li data-full=\"17. The board&#x27;s role: oversight, not micromanagement\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s17\" title=\"17. The board&#x27;s role: oversight, not micromanagement\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>The board's oversight role</button></li><li data-full=\"18. The 1MDB slide: treat it as a governance case\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s18\" title=\"18. The 1MDB slide: treat it as a governance case\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>1MDB as a governance case</button></li><li data-full=\"19. GRC - Governance, Risk and Compliance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s19\" title=\"19. GRC - Governance, Risk and Compliance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>GRC - Governance, Risk and Compliance</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Standards vs regulations - do NOT confuse them\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s20\" title=\"20. Standards vs regulations - do NOT confuse them\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Standards vs regulations</button></li><li data-full=\"21. Why standards exist\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s21\" title=\"21. Why standards exist\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Why standards exist</button></li><li data-full=\"22. Regulatory bodies in the chapter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s22\" title=\"22. Regulatory bodies in the chapter\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Regulatory bodies in the chapter</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Malaysian risk-management requirements - know the institutional mapping\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s23\" title=\"23. Malaysian risk-management requirements - know the institutional mapping\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Malaysian institutional mapping</button></li><li data-full=\"24. SOX: understand WHY it emerged\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s24\" title=\"24. SOX: understand WHY it emerged\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Why SOX emerged</button></li><li data-full=\"25. SOX is fundamentally about trust in information\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s25\" title=\"25. SOX is fundamentally about trust in information\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>SOX and trust in information</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Controls are central to SOX thinking\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s26\" title=\"26. Controls are central to SOX thinking\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Controls are central to SOX thinking</button></li><li data-full=\"27. But controls are not magical\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s27\" title=\"27. But controls are not magical\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>But controls are not magical</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Malaysian regulatory framework\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s28\" title=\"28. Malaysian regulatory framework\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Malaysian regulatory framework</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Evolution of the Malaysian governance framework\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s29\" title=\"29. Evolution of the Malaysian governance framework\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Evolution of the Malaysian governance framework</button></li><li data-full=\"30. COSO - think of it as a structured ERM architecture\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s30\" title=\"30. COSO - think of it as a structured ERM architecture\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>COSO as ERM architecture</button></li><li data-full=\"31. The COSO cube - understand it rather than memorise the picture\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s31\" title=\"31. The COSO cube - understand it rather than memorise the picture\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>The COSO cube</button></li><li data-full=\"32. The eight COSO components shown in your slide\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s32\" title=\"32. The eight COSO components shown in your slide\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Eight COSO components</button></li><li data-full=\"33. Internal environment: &quot;What kind of organisation are we?&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s33\" title=\"33. Internal environment: &quot;What kind of organisation are we?&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Internal environment</button></li><li data-full=\"34. Objective setting: risk requires an objective\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s34\" title=\"34. Objective setting: risk requires an objective\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Objective setting</button></li><li data-full=\"35. Event identification\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s35\" title=\"35. Event identification\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Event identification</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Risk assessment\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s36\" title=\"36. Risk assessment\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Risk assessment</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Risk response\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s37\" title=\"37. Risk response\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Risk response</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Control activities\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s38\" title=\"38. Control activities\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Control activities</button></li><li data-full=\"39. Information and communication\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s39\" title=\"39. Information and communication\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Information and communication</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Monitoring\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s40\" title=\"40. Monitoring\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Monitoring</button></li><li data-full=\"41. ISO 31000 - different philosophy, same core goal\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s41\" title=\"41. ISO 31000 - different philosophy, same core goal\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>ISO 31000 overview</button></li><li data-full=\"42. ISO principles - don&#x27;t memorise eleven isolated sentences\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s42\" title=\"42. ISO principles - don&#x27;t memorise eleven isolated sentences\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>ISO principles in five ideas</button></li><li data-full=\"43. &quot;Risk management is part of decision making&quot; is perhaps ISO&#x27;s most important principle\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s43\" title=\"43. &quot;Risk management is part of decision making&quot; is perhaps ISO&#x27;s most important principle\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>RM is part of decision making</button></li><li data-full=\"44. ISO framework - organisationalising risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s44\" title=\"44. ISO framework - organisationalising risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>ISO framework components</button></li><li data-full=\"45. Why &quot;mandate and commitment&quot; comes first\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s45\" title=\"45. Why &quot;mandate and commitment&quot; comes first\"><span class=\"toc-no\">45</span>Mandate and commitment first</button></li><li data-full=\"46. ISO 31010 and Guide 73\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s46\" title=\"46. ISO 31010 and Guide 73\"><span class=\"toc-no\">46</span>ISO 31010 and Guide 73</button></li><li data-full=\"47. AS/NZS 4360 - process-oriented risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s47\" title=\"47. AS/NZS 4360 - process-oriented risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">47</span>AS/NZS 4360 process</button></li><li data-full=\"48. Why &quot;establish context&quot; comes before risk identification\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s48\" title=\"48. Why &quot;establish context&quot; comes before risk identification\"><span class=\"toc-no\">48</span>Establish context first</button></li><li data-full=\"49. AS/NZS objective: the real philosophy\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s49\" title=\"49. AS/NZS objective: the real philosophy\"><span class=\"toc-no\">49</span>AS/NZS objective</button></li><li data-full=\"50. COSO vs ISO 31000 - don&#x27;t reduce this to &quot;both manage risk&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s50\" title=\"50. COSO vs ISO 31000 - don&#x27;t reduce this to &quot;both manage risk&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">50</span>COSO vs ISO 31000</button></li><li data-full=\"51. SOX vs COSO vs ISO - this comparison is extremely useful\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s51\" title=\"51. SOX vs COSO vs ISO - this comparison is extremely useful\"><span class=\"toc-no\">51</span>SOX vs COSO vs ISO table</button></li><li data-full=\"52. A crucial higher-level insight: compliance is not the same as good risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s52\" title=\"52. A crucial higher-level insight: compliance is not the same as good risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">52</span>Compliance is not good RM</button></li><li data-full=\"53. Another major distinction: controls vs governance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s53\" title=\"53. Another major distinction: controls vs governance\"><span class=\"toc-no\">53</span>Controls vs governance</button></li><li data-full=\"54. Controls vs risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s54\" title=\"54. Controls vs risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">54</span>Controls vs risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"55. A difficult integrated case\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s55\" title=\"55. A difficult integrated case\"><span class=\"toc-no\">55</span>A difficult integrated case</button></li><li data-full=\"56. Now apply COSO\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s56\" title=\"56. Now apply COSO\"><span class=\"toc-no\">56</span>Now apply COSO</button></li><li data-full=\"57. Apply ISO 31000 to the same acquisition\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s57\" title=\"57. Apply ISO 31000 to the same acquisition\"><span class=\"toc-no\">57</span>Apply ISO 31000 to the same acquisition</button></li><li data-full=\"58. Evaluate the board\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s58\" title=\"58. Evaluate the board\"><span class=\"toc-no\">58</span>Evaluate the board</button></li><li data-full=\"59. Apply this to a 1MDB-style governance question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s59\" title=\"59. Apply this to a 1MDB-style governance question\"><span class=\"toc-no\">59</span>1MDB-style governance question</button></li><li data-full=\"60. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy for Topic 3\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s60\" title=\"60. Bloom&#x27;s Taxonomy for Topic 3\"><span class=\"toc-no\">60</span>Bloom's levels for Topic 3</button></li><li data-full=\"61. The three frameworks in one mental map\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s61\" title=\"61. The three frameworks in one mental map\"><span class=\"toc-no\">61</span>The three frameworks in one mental map</button></li><li data-full=\"62. The entire chapter in one system\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s62\" title=\"62. The entire chapter in one system\"><span class=\"toc-no\">62</span>The entire chapter in one system</button></li><li data-full=\"63. The single most important idea to carry forward\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t3n-s63\" title=\"63. The single most important idea to carry forward\"><span class=\"toc-no\">63</span>The idea to carry forward</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l3\" open><summary><div><strong>Topic 3 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with worked examples and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>63 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>Topic 3 is where Topics 1 and 2 finally become <strong>institutionalised</strong>.</p>\n<p>Topic 1 asked:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>What is risk?</span></div>\n<p>Topic 2 asked:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>How should an enterprise manage risk?</span></div>\n<p>Topic 3 asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Who makes sure the organisation actually does it properly?</span></div>\n<p>That is why this chapter moves into <strong>corporate governance, risk governance, standards, internal controls, regulation, SOX, COSO, ISO 31000 and the Malaysian regulatory framework</strong>. Those are the four main areas identified by the chapter itself.</p>\n<p>The master mental model I would use is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Objectives → Risks → Governance → Risk Management Framework → Controls → Monitoring → Accountability\"><span>Objectives</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risks</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Governance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Management Framework</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Controls</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Monitoring</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Accountability</span></div>\n<p>The deepest idea is this:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Risk management tells the organisation how to deal with uncertainty. Governance determines who has authority, who is accountable, what rules apply, and how management is prevented from simply marking its own homework.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. Why do we need governance if we already have management?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides define corporate governance as the structures, policies and laws affecting how a corporation is directed, administered and controlled in the interests of stakeholders.</p>\n<p>That sounds abstract, so strip it down.</p>\n<p>Imagine a company has RM1 billion.</p>\n<p>Management decides how to use it.</p>\n<p>But who decides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>whether management may take RM500 million of debt?</li>\n<li>whether the CEO's performance is acceptable?</li>\n<li>whether financial statements are truthful?</li>\n<li>whether conflicts of interest exist?</li>\n<li>whether risk-taking is excessive?</li>\n<li>whether executives can approve transactions benefiting themselves?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the answer is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Management decides whether management is behaving properly,\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>you have designed a governance system with all the sophistication of leaving a biscuit jar under the supervision of a five-year-old.</p>\n<p>Governance exists because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Authority requires oversight</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Management vs governance - one of the most important distinctions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t3_gov_vs_mgmt\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Governance sits one level above execution: authority, oversight and accountability.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your slides make the distinction:</p>\n<p><strong>Management</strong> deals with daily operations.</p>\n<p><strong>Governance</strong> concerns the system of laws, rules, processes and ethical structures through which the firm is directed and controlled.</p>\n<p>The easiest way to remember this is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Management} = \\text{Running the business}\n}$$</div>\n<p>while</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Governance} = \\text{Making sure the business is run properly}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Consider a bank.</p>\n<h4>Management asks:</h4>\n<p>How many loans should we approve?</p>\n<p>How should branches operate?</p>\n<p>How should employees be allocated?</p>\n<h4>Governance asks:</h4>\n<p>Who may approve large loans?</p>\n<p>How much credit risk is acceptable?</p>\n<p>Who reviews management's decisions?</p>\n<p>What information must reach the board?</p>\n<p>Who checks whether controls are working?</p>\n<p>So governance is <strong>one level above ordinary execution</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Governance and management affect each other</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides also note that poor management can affect governance and weak governance can damage both operational/financial performance and investor confidence.</p>\n<p>This means they are different, but not independent.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Board oversight is weak}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Management takes excessive risks.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Financial losses occur.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Investors lose confidence.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Funding becomes more expensive.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Business performance deteriorates.</p>\n<p>Governance failure therefore becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>financial + strategic + reputational risk</span></div>\n<p>That is already a Topic 1-3 connection.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. The objective of corporate governance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides say governance aims to enhance shareholder value while taking the interests of other stakeholders into consideration.</p>\n<p>That creates a useful tension.</p>\n<p>A company should generate value for owners.</p>\n<p>But it operates among:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>employees,</li>\n<li>customers,</li>\n<li>regulators,</li>\n<li>creditors,</li>\n<li>suppliers,</li>\n<li>communities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Suppose a factory can increase profit by ignoring environmental standards.</p>\n<p>Short term:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Profit} \\uparrow$$</div>\n<p>But potentially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Regulatory risk} \\uparrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Legal risk} \\uparrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Reputation risk} \\uparrow$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Long-term value} \\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>So good governance is not merely:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Be nice to everyone.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is partly about ensuring that short-term managerial decisions do not destroy long-term organisational value.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. The five elements of corporate governance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter gives five basic elements:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Accountability  </li>\n<li>Transparency  </li>\n<li>Regulatory framework  </li>\n<li>Business ethics and social responsibility  </li>\n<li>Administrative structure.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These should not be memorised as five random nouns.</p>\n<p>They solve five different governance problems.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Accountability solves the \"who is responsible?\" problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a RM100 million project fails.</p>\n<p>Everyone says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Not me.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Finance blames operations.</p>\n<p>Operations blames procurement.</p>\n<p>Procurement blames the supplier.</p>\n<p>Management blames \"market conditions.\"</p>\n<p>Congratulations, the company has discovered the ancient management technique of responsibility evaporation.</p>\n<p>Accountability means someone has clearly defined responsibility for decisions and outcomes.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Authority}\n\\leftrightarrow\n\\text{Responsibility}\n}$$</div>\n<p>If you have authority to take risk, you should also be answerable for it.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Transparency solves the information asymmetry problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Corporate governance becomes useless if decision-makers can hide important information.</p>\n<p>Suppose management knows:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Major project is failing}$$</div>\n<p>but tells shareholders:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{\"Everything is excellent.\"}$$</div>\n<p>The board cannot govern properly because its decisions are based on defective information.</p>\n<p>Transparency therefore means relevant information must be available to those who need it.</p>\n<p>You can think of governance quality as constrained by:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Decision Quality}\n\\leq\n\\text{Information Quality}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Even an excellent board cannot make excellent decisions from deliberately misleading data.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Regulatory framework solves the \"rules of the game\" problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The regulatory framework establishes external rules, laws and requirements.</p>\n<p>This is important because voluntary self-restraint is not always enough.</p>\n<p>If every company could decide its own accounting rules, one firm's:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"RM500 million liability\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>would rapidly become:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Strategic future synergy awaiting monetisation.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Regulation creates common minimum expectations.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Business ethics and social responsibility solve the \"legal but wrong\" problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Not every bad decision is illegal.</p>\n<p>Something may be technically permitted but:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>deceptive,</li>\n<li>exploitative,</li>\n<li>harmful,</li>\n<li>unfair.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That is why governance includes ethics in addition to law.</p>\n<p>A useful distinction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance asks: Can we?</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Ethics asks: Should we?</span></div>\n<p>A strong organisation considers both.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Administrative structure solves the \"who reports to whom?\" problem</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Even excellent policies fail if the organisational structure is chaotic.</p>\n<p>Someone must know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>who owns the risk,</li>\n<li>who monitors it,</li>\n<li>who approves treatments,</li>\n<li>who receives reports,</li>\n<li>who escalates problems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without structure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk identified}$$</div>\n<p>does not necessarily become:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk managed}$$</div>\n<p>because nobody knows whose job it is.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Corporate governance vs risk governance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we narrow the concept.</p>\n<p>Corporate governance governs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>the enterprise</span></div>\n<p>Risk governance focuses specifically on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>how risk is governed</span></div>\n<p>Your slides say risk governance ensures that risk-management and control frameworks are embedded into the organisation's <strong>culture, processes and structure</strong>.</p>\n<p>That word <strong>embedded</strong> is extremely important.</p>\n<p>Bad risk governance:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We have a 95-page risk policy PDF somewhere.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Good risk governance:</p>\n<p>Risk considerations actually influence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>planning,</li>\n<li>budgeting,</li>\n<li>approvals,</li>\n<li>investment,</li>\n<li>compensation,</li>\n<li>reporting,</li>\n<li>strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. What does \"embedded\" really mean?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine a company claims:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Safety is our highest priority.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But employee bonuses are based only on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Production volume}$$</div>\n<p>Workers realise:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More output} = \\text{More bonus}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Stopping production for safety checks} = \\text{Less bonus}$$</div>\n<p>What is the <strong>real</strong> risk culture?</p>\n<p>Not what the poster says.</p>\n<p>The incentive system reveals the true culture.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Risk Governance}\n=\n\\text{Policies + Incentives + Accountability + Behaviour}\n}$$</div>\n<p>not just documentation.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Risk-management framework: the operating architecture</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides define a risk-management framework as an organisational approach for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"identifying → analysing → responding → monitoring\"><span>identifying</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>analysing</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>responding</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>monitoring</span></div>\n<p>risks and opportunities, while supporting strategic and operational objectives.</p>\n<p>Think of a framework as the <strong>operating system</strong> for risk management.</p>\n<p>The framework answers:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Who identifies risks?</p>\n<p>How are they measured?</p>\n<p>What terminology is used?</p>\n<p>Who approves responses?</p>\n<p>When are risks escalated?</p>\n<p>How are they reported?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Without a framework, different departments may define \"high risk\" completely differently.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. The seven building blocks of risk governance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides identify seven building blocks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>corporate governance,</li>\n<li>line management,</li>\n<li>portfolio management,</li>\n<li>risk transfer,</li>\n<li>risk analytics,</li>\n<li>data and technology,</li>\n<li>stakeholder management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Instead of memorising them, understand the logic.</p>\n<h4>Corporate governance</h4>\n<p>Top-down direction.</p>\n<h4>Line management</h4>\n<p>Risk management aligned with business activity.</p>\n<h4>Portfolio management</h4>\n<p>Consider risks collectively rather than one by one.</p>\n<h4>Risk transfer</h4>\n<p>Move inefficient or concentrated exposures elsewhere.</p>\n<h4>Risk analytics</h4>\n<p>Measure and analyse risk.</p>\n<h4>Data and technology</h4>\n<p>Provide the information infrastructure.</p>\n<h4>Stakeholder management</h4>\n<p>Communicate risk transparently.</p>\n<p>Together:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Direction}\n+\n\\text{Execution}\n+\n\\text{Aggregation}\n+\n\\text{Treatment}\n+\n\\text{Measurement}\n+\n\\text{Information}\n+\n\\text{Communication}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That's basically the architecture of modern risk governance.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Portfolio management is especially important</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This links directly to Topic 2.</p>\n<p>Suppose the company has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_1=\\text{FX risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_2=\\text{supplier risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_3=\\text{credit risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_4=\\text{market risk}$$</div>\n<p>Traditional thinking might assess each individually.</p>\n<p>Portfolio thinking asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>What is our combined enterprise exposure?</span></div>\n<p>because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_1,R_2,R_3,R_4$$</div>\n<p>may interact.</p>\n<p>This is ERM thinking rather than silo thinking.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Risk management and governance have a two-way relationship</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides on pages 12-13 effectively show:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Corporate Governance}\n\\leftrightarrow\n\\text{Risk Management}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Governance affects risk management because governance establishes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>oversight,</li>\n<li>policies,</li>\n<li>responsibilities,</li>\n<li>accountability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Risk management affects governance because boards need risk information to govern intelligently.</p>\n<p>Suppose a board approves an acquisition.</p>\n<p>Without risk analysis, the board cannot properly evaluate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{strategic risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{debt risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{integration risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{regulatory risk}$$</div>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Good governance requires good risk information.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. The board's role: oversight, not micromanagement</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Pages 15-17 emphasize the board's oversight responsibility.</p>\n<p>This is important.</p>\n<p>The board should not normally decide:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Machine 14 should be serviced at 3:15 PM.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is management.</p>\n<p>Instead the board asks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Are major enterprise risks understood?</li>\n<li>Are controls adequate?</li>\n<li>Are responsibilities clear?</li>\n<li>Are management incentives appropriate?</li>\n<li>Are risk reports reliable?</li>\n<li>Are major exposures being escalated?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Board} = \\text{Oversight}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Management} = \\text{Execution}}$$</div>\n<p>Confusing the two creates either:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>passive boards, or</li>\n<li>boards interfering in every operational decision.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Neither is particularly impressive governance.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. The 1MDB slide: treat it as a governance case</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 14 asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What went wrong?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>using the 1MDB scandal as the case.</p>\n<p>For this chapter, don't reduce the analysis to:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Fraud happened.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The higher-order question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What governance mechanisms should have prevented, detected or escalated the problem?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Using the chapter's framework, you would investigate:</p>\n<h4>Accountability</h4>\n<p>Who had decision authority?</p>\n<h4>Transparency</h4>\n<p>Were transactions and exposures adequately disclosed?</p>\n<h4>Oversight</h4>\n<p>Were boards and committees effectively challenging management?</p>\n<h4>Internal control</h4>\n<p>Were approvals and reporting controls adequate?</p>\n<h4>Risk governance</h4>\n<p>Were concentrated exposures escalated?</p>\n<h4>Compliance</h4>\n<p>Were relevant legal/regulatory obligations followed?</p>\n<h4>Audit</h4>\n<p>Was independent assurance effective?</p>\n<p>That is how you turn a scandal into a governance analysis rather than tabloid recollection.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. GRC - Governance, Risk and Compliance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t3_grc\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>GRC treats governance, risk and compliance as overlapping parts of one system, not three silos.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Page 18 introduces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>GRC</span></div>\n<p>which combines:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Governance}\n+\n\\text{Risk Management}\n+\n\\text{Compliance}$$</div>\n<p>The slide presents it partly as a response to silo mentality.</p>\n<p>The logic is simple.</p>\n<p>Governance asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Who decides and oversees?}$$</div>\n<p>Risk asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>What uncertainty threatens objectives?</span></div>\n<p>Compliance asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{What rules must we follow?}$$</div>\n<p>These overlap heavily.</p>\n<p>For example, entering a new market requires:</p>\n<h4>Governance</h4>\n<p>Board approval.</p>\n<h4>Risk</h4>\n<p>Assess political, FX and operational risk.</p>\n<h4>Compliance</h4>\n<p>Meet licensing and legal requirements.</p>\n<p>Treating them as entirely separate departments can create duplicated work and blind spots.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Standards vs regulations - do NOT confuse them</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is a crucial distinction.</p>\n<p>A <strong>standard/framework</strong> generally tells you:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>How good practice may be structured</span></div>\n<p>A <strong>law/regulation</strong> tells you:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>What you are legally required to do</span></div>\n<p>Examples from this chapter:</p>\n<h4>SOX</h4>\n<p>Law/regulatory requirement in the US context.</p>\n<h4>ISO 31000</h4>\n<p>Risk-management standard/guidance.</p>\n<h4>COSO</h4>\n<p>Framework.</p>\n<h4>Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance</h4>\n<p>Governance code/best-practice framework in the Malaysian context presented by the slides.</p>\n<p>These things are not interchangeable.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Why standards exist</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine every company independently invents its own risk-management process.</p>\n<p>Company A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk}=Probability\\times Impact$$</div>\n<p>Company B:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Red means bad.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Company C:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Risk is whatever the CEO says.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Company D:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"We have no risks.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Some common structure becomes useful.</p>\n<p>Standards therefore help create:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>shared terminology,</li>\n<li>systematic processes,</li>\n<li>comparability,</li>\n<li>completeness,</li>\n<li>governance discipline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Following a framework mechanically does not guarantee good risk management.</span></div>\n<p>A terrible organisation can have beautiful templates.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Regulatory bodies in the chapter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides distinguish international and Malaysian bodies/frameworks.</p>\n<p>International examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ASX,</li>\n<li>Russian corporate governance principles,</li>\n<li>SOX,</li>\n<li>AS/NZS 4360.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For Malaysia, the chapter highlights:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Securities Commission,</li>\n<li>Bursa Malaysia,</li>\n<li>Bank Negara Malaysia,</li>\n<li>Companies Commission of Malaysia/SSM,</li>\n<li>Malaysian governance requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Again, the exam may not merely ask you to list them.</p>\n<p>The stronger question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Which body regulates which type of organisation?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Malaysian risk-management requirements - know the institutional mapping</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 27 gives a very useful table.</p>\n<h4>Bank Negara Malaysia</h4>\n<p>Relevant to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Financial Institutions</span></div>\n<p>and associated with governance/risk-governance guidelines.</p>\n<h4>Securities Commission</h4>\n<p>Associated with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance</span></div>\n<p>for public listed companies.</p>\n<h4>Bursa Malaysia</h4>\n<p>Provides corporate governance and risk/internal-control guidance for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Public Listed Companies</span></div>\n<h4>Putrajaya Committee on GLC High Performance</h4>\n<p>Associated with the Green Book for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Government-Linked Companies</span></div>\n<p>This mapping is much more useful than memorising agency names with no context.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. SOX: understand WHY it emerged</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 came after major corporate fraud cases including Enron and WorldCom. The slides describe its objectives as strengthening controls, financial disclosures, accountability and penalties.</p>\n<p>The logic is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Corporate scandals}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{investor trust collapses}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>existing governance mechanisms judged insufficient</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>stronger regulation</span></div>\n<p>This is a recurring pattern in financial regulation:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Failure → Loss of Trust → Regulatory Response\"><span>Failure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Loss of Trust</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Regulatory Response</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. SOX is fundamentally about trust in information</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides state its objective includes protecting investors by improving the accuracy and reliability of corporate disclosures and deterring corporate/accounting fraud.</p>\n<p>Why does financial disclosure matter so much?</p>\n<p>Investors make decisions based on information.</p>\n<p>Suppose true profit is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM10m$$</div>\n<p>but management reports:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM100m$$</div>\n<p>Investors cannot correctly value the company.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bad Information → Bad Capital Allocation\"><span>Bad Information</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Bad Capital Allocation</span></div>\n<p>SOX therefore attacks a fundamental market problem:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Information asymmetry</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Controls are central to SOX thinking</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Consider revenue reporting.</p>\n<p>Without controls:</p>\n<p>Employee creates fake sales.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Management reports fake revenue.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Profit appears higher.</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>Investors are misled.</p>\n<p>A control might require:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Recorded Revenue}\n\\leftrightarrow\n\\text{Invoice}\n\\leftrightarrow\n\\text{Delivery Evidence}\n\\leftrightarrow\n\\text{Customer Confirmation}$$</div>\n<p>Now manipulating revenue becomes harder.</p>\n<p>That is what <strong>internal control</strong> means conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>A mechanism designed to reduce the chance or impact of error, fraud or undesirable behaviour.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. But controls are not magical</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose every payment above RM1 million requires two signatures.</p>\n<p>Sounds strong.</p>\n<p>But what if the CEO and CFO collude?</p>\n<p>Control:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Two signatures}$$</div>\n<p>still passes.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Controls reduce risk; they do not guarantee zero risk.</span></div>\n<p>This is why governance also requires:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>independence,</li>\n<li>audit,</li>\n<li>culture,</li>\n<li>accountability,</li>\n<li>oversight.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Malaysian regulatory framework</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides state that Malaysian companies can manage risk using the Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance and highlight the principle of:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>identifying principal risks and implementing appropriate systems to manage them.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is basically ERM embedded into governance.</p>\n<p>Notice the sequence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Identify principal risks}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Implement systems}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Manage them}$$</div>\n<p>This is a governance obligation, not simply a risk-department exercise.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Evolution of the Malaysian governance framework</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Page 26 describes the development of the Malaysian Code on Corporate Governance from its first issue in 2000 through subsequent revisions, including 2007, 2012, 2017 and the 2021 update.</p>\n<p>The later development emphasises stronger:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>board oversight,</li>\n<li>board policies/processes,</li>\n<li>director selection,</li>\n<li>sustainability integration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The evolution itself tells you something important:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Governance frameworks are dynamic</span></div>\n<p>because business risks change.</p>\n<p>Cybersecurity, sustainability, complex supply chains and new technology did not have the same importance decades ago.</p>\n<p>Therefore regulation evolves with the risk environment.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. COSO - think of it as a structured ERM architecture</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter presents COSO ERM in three dimensions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>organisational objectives,</li>\n<li>ERM components,</li>\n<li>levels of the organisation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That three-dimensional idea is the key.</p>\n<p>ERM is not merely:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{a list of risks}$$</div>\n<p>It links:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Objectives</span></div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk-management activities</span></div>\n<p>across:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Organisational levels</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. The COSO cube - understand it rather than memorise the picture</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t3_coso_cube3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">The COSO ERM cube in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · Pick an objective, a component and a level; the matching cell of the COSO cube lights up with an example.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The figure on page 28 shows a cube.</p>\n<p>Think of the cube as answering three questions.</p>\n<h4>Question 1 - What are we trying to achieve?</h4>\n<p>Objectives.</p>\n<h4>Question 2 - What risk-management processes do we use?</h4>\n<p>Components.</p>\n<h4>Question 3 - Where in the organisation does this apply?</h4>\n<p>Entity, division, business unit, subsidiary, etc.</p>\n<p>So conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{COSO}\n=\n\\text{Objectives}\n\\times\n\\text{ERM Components}\n\\times\n\\text{Organisation Levels}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That is why the cube is useful.</p>\n<p>It forces risk management to exist across the enterprise rather than in one risk department.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. The eight COSO components shown in your slide</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The COSO 2004 image on page 28 displays:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Internal environment  </li>\n<li>Objective setting  </li>\n<li>Event identification  </li>\n<li>Risk assessment  </li>\n<li>Risk response  </li>\n<li>Control activities  </li>\n<li>Information and communication  </li>\n<li>Monitoring</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These can be understood as a chain.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. Internal environment: \"What kind of organisation are we?\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Before analysing individual risks, ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the culture?</li>\n<li>How ethical is leadership?</li>\n<li>How is authority assigned?</li>\n<li>How much risk-taking is tolerated?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If culture is rotten:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{excellent procedures}$$</div>\n<p>may still produce:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{poor behaviour}$$</div>\n<p>because employees learn what management actually rewards.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. Objective setting: risk requires an objective</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This connects directly to Topic 1.</p>\n<p>Recall:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk = effect of uncertainty on objectives</span></div>\n<p>Therefore, before identifying risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Define the objective</span></div>\n<p>Suppose the objective is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>increase overseas revenue by 25%.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now relevant risks become clearer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>FX,</li>\n<li>political risk,</li>\n<li>market demand,</li>\n<li>supply chain,</li>\n<li>regulation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without the objective, risk identification becomes vague.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. Event identification</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What could happen?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Positive or negative events may affect objectives.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{New regulation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Supplier bankruptcy}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Cyberattack}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Competitor exits market}$$</div>\n<p>The event itself isn't yet the full risk analysis.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Risk assessment</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now ask:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(\\text{event})$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Impact if event occurs}$$</div>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk Magnitude}\n\\approx\nf(\\text{Likelihood},\\text{Impact})$$</div>\n<p>You may also consider speed, persistence, interdependency, etc., but your chapter's core logic is assessment before response.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Risk response</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Management decides what to do.</p>\n<p>Possible responses from your broader course knowledge include things like:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Avoid}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Reduce}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Transfer}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Accept}$$</div>\n<p>The important point is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Assessment should lead to action</span></div>\n<p>A risk register where every risk is carefully documented and nobody does anything is merely an expensive stationery project.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Control activities</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Controls ensure the response actually operates.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<p>Risk:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Fraudulent payments}$$</div>\n<p>Response:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>reduce fraud.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Control activity:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Dual authorisation}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Payment limits}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Vendor verification}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Audit trail}$$</div>\n<p>Controls operationalise risk responses.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. Information and communication</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Risk information has to reach the right decision-maker.</p>\n<p>Suppose the plant manager knows a reactor is becoming unstable.</p>\n<p>Board:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Our risk dashboard is green.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That's not a measurement success.</p>\n<p>It's an information failure.</p>\n<p>Hence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk information must travel upward, downward and across the organisation.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Monitoring</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Controls deteriorate.</p>\n<p>Risks change.</p>\n<p>Employees leave.</p>\n<p>Systems change.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk management must be continuously reviewed</span></div>\n<p>which links directly back to Topic 2's idea of risk management as a cycle.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. ISO 31000 - different philosophy, same core goal</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t3_iso_parts\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>ISO 31000 is three things: principles, a framework that organises RM, and a process.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Your slides describe ISO 31000:2009 as providing principles and guidance for managing risk across organisational activities, and note that the standard itself is not for certification.</p>\n<p>The useful mental model is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{ISO 31000}\n=\n\\text{Principles}\n+\n\\text{Framework}\n+\n\\text{Process}\n}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter explicitly divides it into those three components.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. ISO principles - don't memorise eleven isolated sentences</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides list eleven principles, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>creates value,</li>\n<li>integral to organisational processes,</li>\n<li>part of decision-making,</li>\n<li>addresses uncertainty,</li>\n<li>systematic and timely,</li>\n<li>based on best available information,</li>\n<li>tailored,</li>\n<li>considers human/cultural factors,</li>\n<li>transparent and inclusive,</li>\n<li>dynamic and responsive,</li>\n<li>supports continual improvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I would group them mentally into five ideas.</p>\n<h4>1. Risk management must create value</h4>\n<p>Otherwise why bother?</p>\n<h4>2. It must be integrated</h4>\n<p>Not an isolated department.</p>\n<h4>3. It must support decisions</h4>\n<p>Not documentation for documentation's sake.</p>\n<h4>4. It must fit reality</h4>\n<p>Tailored, information-based, human/cultural.</p>\n<h4>5. It must adapt</h4>\n<p>Dynamic and continuously improved.</p>\n<p>Much easier than memorising eleven bullet points blindly.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. \"Risk management is part of decision making\" is perhaps ISO's most important principle</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose management wants to build a RM2 billion plant.</p>\n<p>Bad model:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Board approves project.</li>\n<li>Risk team later writes a risk register.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Good model:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Define objective.</li>\n<li>Evaluate risk.</li>\n<li>Analyse alternatives.</li>\n<li>Compare expected value and exposure.</li>\n<li>Decide whether/how to proceed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Risk Management}\n\\not=\n\\text{Post-decision paperwork}\n}$$</div>\n<p>It should influence the decision itself.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. ISO framework - organisationalising risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides identify five framework components:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>mandate and commitment,</li>\n<li>design,</li>\n<li>implementation,</li>\n<li>monitoring/review,</li>\n<li>continual improvement.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Commit</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Design</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Implement</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Review</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Improve</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<p>back to the cycle.</p>\n<p>This is closely related to continuous-improvement thinking.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">45. Why \"mandate and commitment\" comes first</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine the CEO says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Risk management is very important.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>no budget,</li>\n<li>no staff,</li>\n<li>no board attention,</li>\n<li>no reporting authority,</li>\n<li>no consequences for ignoring controls.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Is there genuine commitment?</p>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>Hence successful risk management requires leadership commitment.</p>\n<p>Otherwise the framework is ornamental.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">46. ISO 31010 and Guide 73</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides identify related materials:</p>\n<h4>ISO/IEC 31010</h4>\n<p>Risk-assessment techniques.</p>\n<h4>ISO Guide 73</h4>\n<p>Risk-management vocabulary.</p>\n<p>Intuitively:</p>\n<p>ISO 31000 says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How should risk management be structured?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>31010 helps answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What techniques can we use to assess risk?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Guide 73 helps ensure:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Are we speaking the same language?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That distinction is useful.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">47. AS/NZS 4360 - process-oriented risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter also presents AS/NZS 4360 as a generic framework for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identification,</li>\n<li>analysis,</li>\n<li>assessment,</li>\n<li>treatment,</li>\n<li>monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The process shown is essentially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Establish Context}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Identify Risk}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Assess/Prioritise}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Treat}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Monitor/Review}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Communicate/Consult}$$</div>\n<p>around the process.</p>\n<p>That is an intuitive sequence.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s48\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">48. Why \"establish context\" comes before risk identification</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose someone asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is 10% volatility high risk?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Impossible to answer without context.</p>\n<p>For:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>cash reserve → maybe high,</li>\n<li>technology venture → maybe normal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Risk depends on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>objective,</li>\n<li>environment,</li>\n<li>stakeholders,</li>\n<li>constraints.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Context comes first</span></div>\n<p>This is the same logic from Topic 1 again.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">49. AS/NZS objective: the real philosophy</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter says the standard aims to improve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>decision-making,</li>\n<li>opportunity/threat identification,</li>\n<li>value from uncertainty,</li>\n<li>proactive management,</li>\n<li>resource allocation,</li>\n<li>incident management,</li>\n<li>stakeholder confidence,</li>\n<li>compliance,</li>\n<li>corporate governance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice something important:</p>\n<p>It does <strong>not</strong> say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Eliminate all risk.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Instead it talks about:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>better decisions under uncertainty</span></div>\n<p>That is the same advanced interpretation we reached in Topic 2.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s50\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">50. COSO vs ISO 31000 - don't reduce this to \"both manage risk\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>At the conceptual level presented in your slides:</p>\n<h4>COSO</h4>\n<p>Strongly frames ERM around:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Objectives}\n+\n\\text{Components}\n+\n\\text{Organisational Levels}$$</div>\n<h4>ISO 31000</h4>\n<p>Strongly frames risk management around:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Principles}\n+\n\\text{Framework}\n+\n\\text{Process}$$</div>\n<p>So I would remember:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>COSO = enterprise architecture</span></div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ISO 31000 = risk-management principles + integration + process</span></div>\n<p>They overlap heavily, but the conceptual packaging is different.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s51\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">51. SOX vs COSO vs ISO - this comparison is extremely useful</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t3_framework_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Law, framework, standard and code: different nature, overlapping purpose.</figcaption></figure></div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>SOX</th>\n<th>COSO</th>\n<th>ISO 31000</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nature</td>\n<td>Law/regulatory framework</td>\n<td>ERM/internal-control framework</td>\n<td>Risk-management standard/guidance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main concern</td>\n<td>Accountability and reliable corporate reporting</td>\n<td>Structured enterprise risk management</td>\n<td>General risk-management principles/framework/process</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Core question</td>\n<td>Are required controls/disclosures adequate?</td>\n<td>How does ERM operate across objectives and organisation?</td>\n<td>How should risk management be integrated and performed?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Orientation</td>\n<td>Compliance/accountability</td>\n<td>Enterprise-wide</td>\n<td>Generic/adaptable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This is a much better understanding than memorising acronyms.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s52\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">52. A crucial higher-level insight: compliance is not the same as good risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose Company X satisfies every minimum regulatory requirement.</p>\n<p>Does that mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk}=0?$$</div>\n<p>No.</p>\n<p>A company can be legally compliant and still make an idiotic strategic decision.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>acquisition legally approved,</li>\n<li>disclosures properly made,</li>\n<li>accounting correct,</li>\n</ul>\n<p>but management pays twice what the company is worth.</p>\n<p>Compliance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\checkmark$$</div>\n<p>Strategy:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{disaster}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance is necessary but not sufficient for effective ERM.</span></div>\n<p>That is a strong evaluation-level conclusion.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s53\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">53. Another major distinction: controls vs governance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose employees cannot approve their own expense claims.</p>\n<p>That is a:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Control</span></div>\n<p>Who decided the rule, who monitors it, who is accountable, and who oversees exceptions?</p>\n<p>That is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Governance</span></div>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Governance establishes the system;\ncontrols operate within the system.</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s54\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">54. Controls vs risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Risk management asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What might prevent the objective?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Control asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What mechanism will reduce that risk?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<h4>Objective</h4>\n<p>Accurate financial reporting.</p>\n<h4>Risk</h4>\n<p>Revenue manipulation.</p>\n<h4>Control</h4>\n<p>Independent reconciliation.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Objective → Risk → Response → Control\"><span>Objective</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Response</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Control</span></div>\n<p>This sequence is essential.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s55\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">55. A difficult integrated case</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>XYZ Berhad wants to acquire a foreign technology company for RM3 billion. The CEO strongly supports the acquisition. Several directors have close relationships with the target company's owners. Due diligence is rushed. Management forecasts extremely optimistic revenue, while the risk team identifies major cybersecurity and debt-servicing concerns. The board nevertheless approves the deal after receiving only a short presentation.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now analyse this chapter properly.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Objective</h3>\n<p>Management wants growth and shareholder value.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strategic Objective</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Strategic risk</h3>\n<p>The acquisition may fail to generate expected value.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Financial/liquidity risk</h3>\n<p>Debt financing may increase payment pressure.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Cyber risk</h3>\n<p>Target's systems may introduce operational exposure.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Governance risk</h3>\n<p>Directors may have conflicts of interest.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Transparency problem</h3>\n<p>Board information may be incomplete.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Accountability problem</h3>\n<p>It may be unclear who challenged assumptions.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Risk governance failure</h3>\n<p>Risk concerns were identified but not properly integrated into decision-making.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Internal-control issue</h3>\n<p>Due-diligence and approval controls may be inadequate.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Board oversight failure</h3>\n<p>The board approved a major transaction without sufficient challenge.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s56\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">56. Now apply COSO</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Internal environment</h4>\n<p>Is there a culture where management can be challenged?</p>\n<h4>Objective setting</h4>\n<p>What value is the acquisition supposed to create?</p>\n<h4>Event identification</h4>\n<p>Cyber problems, integration failure, declining demand, debt pressure.</p>\n<h4>Risk assessment</h4>\n<p>Likelihood and impact.</p>\n<h4>Risk response</h4>\n<p>Reduce debt, negotiate price, strengthen cyber controls, perhaps abandon transaction.</p>\n<h4>Control activities</h4>\n<p>Independent due diligence, conflict declarations, approval requirements.</p>\n<h4>Information and communication</h4>\n<p>Full risk report reaches board.</p>\n<h4>Monitoring</h4>\n<p>Post-acquisition performance monitored.</p>\n<p>Now COSO is no longer a cube you memorised.</p>\n<p>It becomes a decision framework.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s57\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">57. Apply ISO 31000 to the same acquisition</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Creates value</h4>\n<p>Does risk management improve the decision?</p>\n<h4>Part of decision-making</h4>\n<p>Risk assessment happens <strong>before approval</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Best available information</h4>\n<p>Use credible due diligence.</p>\n<h4>Tailored</h4>\n<p>Assessment matches technology/acquisition risks.</p>\n<h4>Human and cultural factors</h4>\n<p>Consider management optimism and conflicts.</p>\n<h4>Transparent and inclusive</h4>\n<p>Relevant concerns reach the board.</p>\n<h4>Dynamic</h4>\n<p>Update risks as acquisition negotiations change.</p>\n<p>Now you understand why ISO principles exist.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s58\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">58. Evaluate the board</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A weak answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The board should not approve because it is risky.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Everything interesting is risky.</p>\n<p>A better answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The board should not approve the acquisition <strong>in its current information state</strong>, because material conflicts, inadequate due diligence, cybersecurity exposure and debt risks prevent the board from making a sufficiently informed decision.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Then recommend:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>independent due diligence,</li>\n<li>conflict-of-interest management,</li>\n<li>revised valuation,</li>\n<li>debt stress testing,</li>\n<li>cyber assessment,</li>\n<li>formal risk report,</li>\n<li>reconsideration of the transaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That is Bloom's <strong>Evaluate</strong> level.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s59\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">59. Apply this to a 1MDB-style governance question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If an exam asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Using corporate governance principles, analyse what can go wrong in a major investment scandal.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Do not merely write:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Accountability, transparency, regulation, ethics, administrative structure.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Instead:</p>\n<h4>Accountability</h4>\n<p>Was authority matched with responsibility?</p>\n<h4>Transparency</h4>\n<p>Were material exposures and transactions properly communicated?</p>\n<h4>Regulatory framework</h4>\n<p>Were relevant rules followed?</p>\n<h4>Ethics</h4>\n<p>Were conflicts or improper incentives present?</p>\n<h4>Administrative structure</h4>\n<p>Did boards, audit functions and reporting lines operate independently?</p>\n<p>That's the difference between:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Remember}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Analyse</span></div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s60\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">60. Bloom's Taxonomy for Topic 3</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Remember</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>List the five elements of corporate governance.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Accountability, transparency, regulatory framework, ethics/social responsibility, administrative structure.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Understand</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Explain the difference between management and governance.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Management runs daily activities; governance provides oversight, direction, rules and accountability.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Apply</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A company requires two authorised officers for major payments. What is this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A control activity designed to reduce fraud/error risk.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Analyse</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A company has a comprehensive risk framework but directors routinely ignore risk reports. Evaluate the governance problem.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The framework exists formally, but risk governance is weak because risk information is not embedded into decision-making and oversight.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Evaluate</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is regulatory compliance enough to demonstrate strong ERM?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>No. Compliance may satisfy minimum legal obligations but does not ensure strategic, operational or emerging risks are appropriately managed.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Create</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Design a governance system for a listed company.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You could construct:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Shareholders}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Board}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Audit/Risk Committees}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Management}$$</div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk Owners}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Policies}\n+\n\\text{Controls}\n+\n\\text{Reporting}\n+\n\\text{Assurance}\n+\n\\text{Monitoring}$$</div>\n<p>That is creation-level thinking.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s61\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">61. The three frameworks in one mental map</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For revision, use this:</p>\n<h4>SOX</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fraud/Reporting Failures → Stronger Accountability + Controls + Disclosure\"><span>Fraud/Reporting Failures</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Stronger Accountability + Controls + Disclosure</span></div>\n<h4>COSO</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Objectives}\n\\times\n\\text{ERM Components}\n\\times\n\\text{Organisation}\n}$$</div>\n<h4>ISO 31000</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Principles}\n+\n\\text{Framework}\n+\n\\text{Process}\n}$$</div>\n<h4>Malaysian framework</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>SC + Bursa + BNM + SSM + MCCG + relevant legislation/guidelines</span></div>\n<p>Each solves a slightly different governance/risk-management problem.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s62\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">62. The entire chapter in one system</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You can compress Topic 3 into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>CORPORATE GOVERNANCE</span></div>\n<p>Who directs, controls and oversees the organisation?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>RISK GOVERNANCE</span></div>\n<p>Who governs risk-taking and risk management?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>RISK MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK</span></div>\n<p>How are risks identified, assessed, treated and monitored?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>CONTROLS</span></div>\n<p>What mechanisms reduce the risks?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>STANDARDS</span></div>\n<p>What recognised structure/good practices can be followed?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>REGULATION</span></div>\n<p>What must legally or regulatorily be done?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ASSURANCE + MONITORING</span></div>\n<p>How do we know the system actually works?</p>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ACCOUNTABILITY</span></div>\n<p>Who answers when it does not?</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t3n-s63\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">63. The single most important idea to carry forward</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Do <strong>not</strong> come away from Topic 3 thinking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Governance means following rules.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is too shallow.</p>\n<p>A stronger interpretation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Governance is the architecture that determines how power, information, accountability and risk-taking are controlled inside an organisation.</span></div>\n<p>And:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Standards such as COSO and ISO help structure that architecture, while regulation establishes mandatory boundaries around organisational behaviour.</span></div>\n<p>That gives you the progression of the first three chapters:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Topic 1: Understand Risk</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Topic 2: Manage Risk Enterprise-Wide</span></div>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Topic 3: Govern, Standardise and Regulate Risk Management</span></div>\n<p>Once you see that sequence, Topic 3 stops looking like a pile of acronyms and legislation and starts looking like the answer to a much more practical question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How do we prevent an organisation from taking material risks irresponsibly, hiding them, misunderstanding them, or leaving nobody accountable when they go wrong?</strong></p>\n</blockquote></div></details></div></details>");
